WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.159 --> 00:00:03.920
Speaker 1: Let's go next to Dylan&nbsp;
from Massachusetts. Dylan, welcome.&nbsp;

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:07.360
Speaker 3: Hi there. How's it going?
Speaker 1: It's going well.&nbsp;

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:13.600
Speaker 3: That's good to hear. So if&nbsp;
I didn't were to, by executive order,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:19.120
set up abortion clinics on federal land in&nbsp;
states that have banned abortion. Do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:23.760
think that there's anything that those states&nbsp;
can do to stop those clinics from functioning?&nbsp;

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:29.840
Speaker 1: You know, I'm sure that there are&nbsp;
things they can try to do with new arguments that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:36.720
we've not heard used before. But it would be sort&nbsp;
of like a VA hospital in a sense. Right. Where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:42.800
when in fact, I remember a story from growing up&nbsp;
in Massachusetts where at a VA hospital there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:48.000
like a nurse that killed a bunch of patients&nbsp;
by deliberately giving them too much or the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:55.200
wrong medication. And it was subject to the death&nbsp;
penalty, even though Massachusetts didn't have it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:59.840
because it happened in a federal facility. And&nbsp;
I believe that along the same lines, if federal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:04.480
facilities wanted to offer abortion services, even&nbsp;
in states that have banned it, I think it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:10.160
be very difficult for the state to supersede&nbsp;
or come in and do anything about it. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:14.720
it would have to be tested and there would be a&nbsp;
test, but it sounds like it would be pretty solid.&nbsp;

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:20.320
Speaker 3: Hmm. Yeah, because I've heard&nbsp;
arguments from people who say that that's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:26.240
bad idea for Biden to do that by executive order.&nbsp;
Like, for example, if a state were to arrest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:33.440
like someone like a doctor going on site&nbsp;
before or after they leave the federal land.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:39.680
Yep. For having committed the crime of&nbsp;
doing an abortion procedure on federal land.&nbsp;

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:43.360
Speaker 1: That would get very dicey. That&nbsp;
would that would get extremely dicey because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:48.560
it's not like a it's not like an embassy.&nbsp;
Right. A VA hospital or a federal hospital.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:55.680
It's not like an embassy where literally no&nbsp;
rules apply. But, yeah, I mean, it's a it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:01.200
really a complicated legal question.
Speaker 3: Yeah. If you if you could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:06.560
snap your fingers and compel Joe Biden to set&nbsp;
up these VA abortion clinics on federal land,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:12.720
would you or would you not compel him to do that?
Speaker 1: I'd have to think about it. I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:16.560
really have to think about it. I mean, the ideal&nbsp;
thing would be to codify Roe v Wade. But that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:20.160
not up to Biden. That's up to Democrats&nbsp;
in the Senate. They don't have the votes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:23.840
I would want to know more about it before&nbsp;
I took a really strong position on it.&nbsp;

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:32.880
Speaker 3: I'm of the same opinion. Do you do you&nbsp;
think if Democrats get the votes after, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:37.360
by some miracle, this midterm elections,&nbsp;
do you think that the Supreme Court would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:41.920
allow Roe to be codified into law?
Speaker 1: I from what I've read,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:44.800
it would be very difficult for&nbsp;
the Supreme Court to stop it.&nbsp;

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:49.840
Speaker 3: Now. All right. Well,&nbsp;
thanks for taking my call, David.&nbsp;

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:53.600
Speaker 1: All right. Dylan from&nbsp;
Massachusetts, very much appreciate the call.

